Subject: [PATCH 17/21] coreaudio: start playback at the target, past a startup
 burst

When playback starts, the server sends the audio it has queued at once,
while the output unit is still being set up: the ring then held ~480 ms
instead of the ~90 ms target, and the sound ran that late until the
once-a-second skip caught up. The delay reported meanwhile (650-670 ms with
AirPods) also reached spice-server's GStreamer rate control as the stream's
best video and audio margin, and every later report (~280 ms) then read as
half of it: it lowered the bit rate report after report and dropped frames
itself. More than the target (plus the skip slack) buffered when the output
(re)starts is now skipped there and then.
---
 src/spice-coreaudio.c | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/spice-coreaudio.c b/src/spice-coreaudio.c
index 6e48fce..68c6e31 100644
--- a/src/spice-coreaudio.c
+++ b/src/spice-coreaudio.c
@@ -30,8 +30,11 @@
  * time the output runs dry anyway; after every 5 calm minutes it shrinks by
  * 10 ms, down to where it started. Audio that stays buffered beyond the
  * target for a whole second (after a burst, or as the guest's and the Mac's
- * clocks drift) is skipped, so the delay does not grow. The delay reported to
- * the session is what is really buffered plus the output device's own latency.
+ * clocks drift) is skipped, so the delay does not grow; more than the target
+ * buffered when the output (re)starts — the audio the server sends at once when
+ * playback starts, while the unit is still being set up — is skipped then and
+ * there. The delay reported to the session is what is really buffered plus the
+ * output device's own latency.
  *
  * CoreAudio calls that can block — starting, stopping and creating units and
  * queues, which waits for the device — run on a serial dispatch queue, not on
@@ -538,6 +541,22 @@ static void playback_data(SpicePlaybackChannel *channel, gpointer *audio, gint s
 
     if (atomic_load(&pb->starved) && atomic_load(&pb->configuring) == 0 &&
         used >= atomic_load(&pb->target)) {
+        guint target = atomic_load(&pb->target);
+        guint frame_bytes = atomic_load(&pb->frame_bytes);
+        guint slack = SKIP_SLACK_MS * atomic_load(&pb->rate) / 1000 * frame_bytes;
+
+        /* Start from the newest `target`: a startup burst left in the ring
+         * delayed the sound by ~0.5 s for a second, and that delay, reported,
+         * made spice-server's rate control judge every later margin as half of
+         * what the stream had had, and lower the video bit rate for good. */
+        if (frame_bytes > 0 && used > target + slack) {
+            guint64 skip = used - target;
+            skip -= skip % frame_bytes;
+            atomic_store(&pb->flush_to, w + size - (used - skip));
+            atomic_fetch_add(&pb->skipped, (guint)skip);
+            used -= skip;
+            SPICE_DEBUG("coreaudio: starting %u ms in, past a burst", bytes_to_ms(pb, (guint)skip));
+        }
         atomic_store(&pb->starved, FALSE);
         if (!pb->delay_reported) {
